ordet 0.1.0
a quiet terminal word processor

    ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │ one two three                                │
    │                                              │
    │ Insert │ Pg 1/1 │ L1 C14 │ W:3 C:13          │
    └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Ordet takes its name from Kaj Munk's play and Carl Theodor Dreyer's film.
It is built in Rust on its own terminal UI framework, for writers,
developers, terminal and Rust enthusiasts, and arthouse cinephiles.

It gives you paragraph and character styles, a visible ruler, tiled
windows, search and replace, block editing, bookmarks, autosave, crash
recovery, revision history, backups, and a system clipboard bridge. Save
as .ord or plain text. Every keystroke inserts exactly what was typed.
Ordet makes no network requests.

[ install ]

Requires a stable Rust toolchain:

    git clone https://github.com/LeonardoAmadeo/ordet.git
    cd ordet
    cargo install --path crates/ordet --locked

[ use ]

    ordet
    ordet draft.ord notes.txt

Use --config <path> for a different settings file, --print-config to print
the effective settings, --reset-config to write defaults, and -h / -V for
help and version information.

[ keys ]

F1 opens the shortcut guide. F2 opens settings. F10 opens the menu bar.
Ctrl-Q quits. Ctrl-K and Ctrl-O are the two command prefixes, and the full
reference lives in F1.

Pages are drafts. Alt-N opens the next page in a split, F6 cycles pages and
windows, and Alt-Up / Alt-Down reorder the page list. Panels are modal and
close with Esc. Themes and every other preference live under F2.

[ platforms ]

- macOS: primary development platform, fully tested.
- Linux: Ubuntu CI build and test; interactive X11/Wayland testing is
  still pending.
- Windows: experimental. Native CI builds and tests ordet.exe. Use Windows
  Terminal or WezTerm with VT input. Interactive testing is still pending.

